---
/**
 * CHARGING IT TO THE GAME — slice R9.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md Section Composition table, row "Charging It to the Game":
 *
 *   Entrance      DELIBERATELY BREAKS THE STAIRCASE. This section and
 *                 the Feature Quote are the page's two sanctioned
 *                 exceptions to the three-left-edge system.
 *   Column window BESIDE (>=1255): kicker 1-2 · LEDE 3-11, its own
 *                 band across the top · turn/body/coda 3-8 · frame
 *                 9-12, in the turn's row rather than the section's
 *                 top edge.   STACKED (<=1254): kicker 1-2 · lede and
 *                 type 3-9 · coda to the right trim · frame beneath
 *                 the coda.   (R9.5 amends the R9 row, and CONTEXT.md's
 *                 Composition table already records the amendment.)
 *   Image         `column` — lifestyle-street, right side
 *   Rhythm        open. 110px top, 40px bottom — the table's own
 *                 per-section value for this row, flat at every width.
 *   Length        manifesto only; the coda's three lines hang on one
 *                 edge.
 *
 * TWO LEFT EDGES, NOT THREE. Everywhere else on the page the kicker
 * outdents, the lede takes the body edge and the body indents one
 * column in from it. Here the kicker outdents to columns 1-2 and the
 * lede, the turn, the body AND the coda all share ONE edge at column
 * 3. The body does not indent.
 *
 * R9.5 DOES NOT ADD AN EDGE. The lede band widens to column 11 but
 * still STARTS on column 3. Only its right trim moved, which is how
 * CONTEXT.md's R4.1 image-paired amendment says a single-type-edge
 * section differentiates its registers — "on the RIGHT edge instead."
 *
 * COLUMN 3 IS A SANCTIONED FOURTH EDGE, FOR THIS SECTION ONLY, AND
 * MUST NOT BE PROPAGATED. CONTEXT.md's standing rule — "three left
 * edges must stay legible, which means nothing may land on a fourth"
 * — is what makes this a decision rather than a drift. What keeps it
 * legible as a step-out rather than as column 4 missed by one is that
 * the section ALSO drops the body indent entirely. One type edge one
 * column left of the page's lede edge reads as a section that has
 * moved; one type edge at 3 with a second at 4 would read as an
 * error. The two changes only work together, which is why neither
 * ships without the other.
 *
 * NO DISPLAY-SCALE SECTION TITLE. The section's name lives in the
 * kicker, as it does on Educational Leader. The lede is the only
 * display-scale type in the section.
 *
 * MANIFESTO ONLY. No posts exist and no blog URL exists. COPY.md:
 * "No post index, no fabricated articles, no 'coming soon'
 * placeholder." There is also no button — every other pointing
 * section on this page points AT something, and this one has nothing
 * to point at. COPY.md's Appendix A3 holds two cut paragraphs for
 * this section; they do not ship and their absence is not an
 * oversight.
 *
 * NO RULES, AND THEREFORE NO GOLD. This section draws no hairline
 * anywhere — not under the head, not above the coda. Educational
 * Leader's coda takes a hairline lid at columns 4-6; importing that
 * device into the one section built to break the pattern would
 * defeat the break. CONTEXT.md's gold budget is untouched by this
 * slice: 3 of 3 saturated accents (all elsewhere), 0 of 8 hairline
 * contexts.
 *
 * THE <h2> IS THE KICKER AND IT IS VISIBLE. CONTEXT.md's locked
 * heading structure: each section's <h2> is the element whose text
 * names the section, and that string equals the folio label.
 * Verified against src/data/sections.ts — { id:
 * 'charging-it-to-the-game', n: '07', label: 'Charging It to the
 * Game' } — not assumed. The id is the same string the Index's entry
 * 05 has been linking to since R8; this component is what makes that
 * anchor land.
 *
 * COLLAPSE, IN FOUR BANDS, STATED BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION. The
 * primitives carrying it live in global.css beside every other
 * window; nothing in this file hand-sets a grid-column.
 *
 *   band 1  >=1280     kicker 1-2 row 1 · LEDE 3-11 row 1 · type
 *                      3-8 row 2 · frame 9-12 row 2, beside the type
 *   band 2  1024-1279  the same, down to the CROSSOVER AT 1254;
 *                      below it the lede REJOINS the type window at
 *                      3-9, the type widens to 3-9, and the frame
 *                      drops BENEATH the coda at 3-7 on the type's
 *                      own left edge
 *   band 3  751-1023   kicker full measure · lede full measure, trim
 *                      to trim · type 1-5 above 900 and 1-6 at 900 and
 *                      below · frame beneath the coda at 1-5, on the
 *                      type's own left edge, which is column 1
 *   band 4  <=750      one column, everything full measure in reading
 *                      order: kicker, lede, turn, body, coda, frame
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT. No observer, no listener, no reveal, no
 * opacity: 0 initial state. Every word is in the DOM and visible on
 * load, which is what makes the section survive a full-page capture
 * and any PDF the client generates.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import lifestyleStreet from '../assets/lifestyle-street-inset.jpg';

/*
 * THE FILE IS `lifestyle-street-inset.jpg`, AND THE NAME IN
 * CONTEXT.md's photography table IS `lifestyle-street.jpg`. There is
 * no file by that name on disk. CONTEXT.md's own filename note is the
 * reason this was checked rather than guessed: raw camera-named files
 * in this project are NOT interchangeable with their working-named
 * counterparts, and `SHP SHoot 7.jpg` is explicitly recorded as a
 * DIFFERENT IMAGE from this one. Verified rather than assumed:
 *
 *   src/assets/lifestyle-street-inset.jpg
 *     sha256 f5c94c3e8f501912a2b68f3066ac325cf5f5acc3e9dfafaa8bb221208bc9f441
 *     sharp().metadata()  1500 x 2100, no EXIF orientation tag
 *     native aspect       0.7143
 *
 *   brand_assets/SHP SHoot 7.jpg
 *     sha256 605db5faff2956c24e5e6c75e4e969cdcd718375c0c94f95ed8880e95f12a148
 *     sharp().metadata()  2832 x 4240, no EXIF orientation tag
 *     native aspect       0.6679
 *
 * Different hash, different dimensions, different aspect. They are
 * two files and only this one is the working asset.
 *
 * sharp().metadata() rather than `sips`, per CONTEXT.md: `sips`
 * reports the raw buffer and was wrong on mcclain-bleachers.jpg,
 * which carries EXIF orientation 6. This file carries no orientation
 * tag at all, so stored and presented dimensions agree — but that is
 * a measurement, not an assumption.
 */
---

<section class="sheet chg" id="charging-it-to-the-game" aria-labelledby="chg-heading">
  <!--
    THE HEAD — outdented to columns 1-2 and top-aligned in its row, so
    the kicker sits on the section's top content edge and hangs to the
    LEFT of the lede rather than above it.

    NO RULE ABOVE IT. Educational Leader opens on a hairline and a
    kicker; two sections cannot announce themselves the same way, and
    this one is the page's designated pattern-break. The outdent alone
    is the entrance.
  -->
  <div class="win-chg-head chg-head">
    <h2 class="chg-kicker" id="chg-heading"><span>Charging It</span> <span>to the Game</span></h2>
    <p class="chg-subtitle">The Blog</p>
  </div>

  <!--
    THE LEDE BAND — R9.5. ITS OWN GRID ITEM, ITS OWN ROW.

    R9 through R9.4 authored the lede as the first child of the type
    block, so it inherited the type window: six columns at 3-8 in the
    beside arrangement. A display line has no business in a body
    measure. At 1440 it set five lines at --lede-size, and every
    correction pass that tried to fix that by SIZING it ran into
    CONTEXT.md's 3x lede-to-body minimum from the other side. The
    window was the variable, not the size.

    It now takes columns 3-11 — its own full-width band across the top
    of the section, above the frame — and the frame drops to sit beside
    the turn, body and coda only.

    IT STILL SHARES THE SECTION'S ONE LEFT EDGE. Column 3 in the
    beside arrangement, column 1 in band 3, the trim in band 4 — the
    same edge the turn, the body and the coda run on. A wider band is
    not a second edge; it is the same edge with a different right trim,
    which is exactly how CONTEXT.md's image-paired sections
    differentiate (R4.1: "its lede, body and closing line differentiate
    on the RIGHT edge instead").

    IN THE STACKED ARRANGEMENT ITS WINDOW DOES NOT MOVE. Below the
    crossover it takes 3-9, byte-for-byte the window the type block
    takes, because there is no frame beside it to make room for. The
    split is a beside-arrangement change; the stacked composition is
    untouched and re-measured to prove it.
  -->
  <p class="win-chg-lede chg-lede">There are lessons that cannot be taught in a classroom, found in a playbook, or measured on a scoreboard.</p>

  <!--
    THE TYPE COLUMN. One block, one left edge, three registers now:
    turn, body, coda. Authored as a single grid item rather than as
    three, because the whole point of this section is that they share
    an edge — separate items on separate placements would be separate
    chances for one of them to drift onto a fifth.
  -->
  <div class="win-chg-text chg-text">
    <!--
      THE TURN. Its own block-level element, set in the BODY face at
      BODY size, with air above and below equal to twice the
      section's largest other internal gap. COPY.md: "its own
      paragraph, set alone. Must not be absorbed above or below."

      IT IS NOT PROMOTED TO A THIRD DISPLAY REGISTER. Fraunces italic
      is the pivot's, and WONK 1 is spent there and nowhere else. What
      sets this paragraph alone is SPACE, not face — which is also the
      only device that cannot be confused with the lede sitting three
      lines above it in the same column.
    -->
    <p class="chg-turn">But this is not simply a football story.</p>

    <div class="chg-body">
      <p>It is about the miles driven when no one was watching. The sacrifices families make behind the scenes. The decisions that change trajectories. The losses that teach more than victories ever could. And the understanding that sometimes the lesson only makes sense after living through it.</p>
    </div>

    <!--
      THE CODA — three hard lines on one edge, the section's type
      edge. THE LINE BREAKS ARE STRUCTURAL: three separate block-level
      elements, never one paragraph relying on a natural rag to fall
      in the right places.

      Never run together as a sentence. Never centred. No decorative
      rules between them, and no rule above them.

      Set in the page's existing closing-line register — the same
      Fraunces opsz 96 / wght 400 / SOFT 35 / WONK 0 at
      clamp(26px, 2.2vw, 32px) that .edu-coda and .ath-close both
      carry. No new size token was introduced for this section.
    -->
    <div class="chg-coda">
      <p>Because some things are wins.</p>
      <p>Some things are lessons.</p>
      <p>And some things you simply <br class="chg-coda-break" />have to charge to the game.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!--
    THE FRAME. `column` tier — half the page or less, inside the body
    window's row rather than breaking a trim. CONTEXT.md allots
    exactly one `bleed-left` and one `inset` on the whole page and
    both are spent on Athletic Management; this is a column plate and
    nothing else.

    NO CAPTION. R9 and R9.1 hung "OFF THE CLOCK" beneath the plate;
    Jackson removed it in R9.2. CONTEXT.md's rule is that every
    photograph in a SECTION carries a caption, with the Cover and
    Premier Leadership as the two enumerated exceptions — so this is a
    THIRD exception and CONTEXT.md's Photography section does not yet
    record it. Flagged in the R9.2 report; that file is not edited
    from here.

    The <figure> stays a <figure>: it is still a self-contained
    illustrative unit referenced from the flow, and a figure with no
    figcaption is well-formed. Removing the caption takes 56px off the
    figure column — the caption's 28px line on a 28px baseline margin
    — which changes which column governs the row and is re-measured
    rather than assumed.

    NO HEIGHT FLOOR AND NO STRETCH. The type column sets the
    section's height and the frame's surplus ground sits below it.
    This is deliberately NOT Athletic's arrangement: there the frame
    carries a minimum aspect and the surplus had to be pushed above
    the type. Here the frame has one intrinsic shape at every width
    and never competes for the row's height.
  -->
  <figure class="win-chg-frame chg-figure">
    <div class="plate-img chg-frame">
      <!--
        `sizes` DESCRIBES THE <img> BOX, NOT THE PLATE. This crop
        oversizes the box VERTICALLY — the rendered aspect is wider
        than the source's, so the window comes off the top, not off
        the sides — which means the box's WIDTH is the plate's width
        and `sizes` is the plate. That is the one case where the two
        agree, and it is stated here so nobody "corrects" it later by
        multiplying in a crop factor that applies to the other axis.
        Measured per band, box width against viewport:

          <=750    plate is the full sheet    670px at 750  = 90vw
          <=1023   plate is 5 of 8, stacked   582px at 1023 = 57vw
          <=1254   plate is 5 of 12, stacked  455px at 1254 = 37vw
          else     plate is 4 of 12, beside   581px at 1920 = 31vw

        THE THIRD BREAKPOINT NAMES THE CROSSOVER AND HAD TO FOLLOW IT
        (R9.2). The crossover moved 1141 -> 1254, and this declaration
        is the one that describes the STACKED plate; left at 1141 the
        widths 1142-1254 would have fallen through to the beside
        figure of 31vw while rendering a stacked plate of up to 455px,
        under-declaring by 60px at 1200 and fetching a rendition the
        layout then upsamples. The VALUES were re-derived only where
        the moved boundary forced it: 36vw no longer covers the band's
        widest instance (455.2px at 1254 is 36.3vw), so it steps to
        37. Everything else in this table is R9's and untouched.

        Each declaration carries the widest instance in its band with
        a point or two of headroom. Source is 1500px wide, so the
        ladder stops there: at 750 the box is 670 CSS px and a 2x
        screen wants 1340, which the file supplies; at 390 it wants
        680 and gets 720. Nothing on this page upsamples.
      -->
      <Image
        src={lifestyleStreet}
        alt="Dr. Syreeta McClain standing in the middle of a downtown street in a red blazer and jeans."
        widths={[480, 720, 960, 1200, 1500]}
        sizes="(max-width: 750px) 90vw, (max-width: 1023px) 57vw, (max-width: 1254px) 37vw, 31vw"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="sync"
      />
    </div>

  </figure>
</section>

<style>
  /* ---- THE SECTION ------------------------------------------------
     110px top, 40px bottom, FLAT AT EVERY WIDTH. Straight from
     CONTEXT.md's per-section padding list, which gives this row
     "Blog 110/40" — the only asymmetric pair on the page. No taper,
     no svh term, no clamp. About's svh term is scoped to a problem
     this section does not have (keeping a lede below an 80svh Cover's
     fold) and copying it here would be cargo-culting.

     The short bottom is the point: the coda's three lines are the
     page's quietest close, and 40px of ground under them hands the
     scroll to Contact rather than pausing on it. */
  .chg{
    padding-block: 110px 40px;

    /* ---- THE CODA'S DERIVATION, R9.1 ----------------------------
       --closing-size    LOCAL ALIAS of the page's shared closing-line
                         register, byte-for-byte what .edu-coda and
                         .ath-close declare. It is the CAP. Declared
                         here so min() can name it; nothing else on the
                         page sees it and neither of those components
                         is touched.
       --chg-coda-k      measured advance-per-px of the coda's longest
                         line at the shipped axes. 20.7752 for the full
                         53-character line 3; band 4 overrides it to
                         13.3720, the value for the authored break's
                         first half, because that is the longest line
                         once the break is live.
       --chg-coda-floor  1.5 x body. A REPORTING TRIPWIRE: where it
                         engages, the width is a failure, not a
                         shipped value.
       --chg-coda-window the coda's rendered window, in closed form
                         against the same grid arithmetic the
                         primitives use. Bands 2-4 override it below
                         and band 3 also SETS its box from it, so the
                         size and the box cannot disagree.

       BAND 1 AND BAND 2 ABOVE THE CROSSOVER: the coda takes the type
       window, columns 3-8 of 12.
           6 cols + 5 gaps = (100vw - 392px)/2 + 120px = 50vw - 76px
       Verified against the rendered window: 884.0 at 1920, 644.0 at
       1440, 564.0 at 1280, 495.0 at 1142. */
    --closing-size:    clamp(26px, 2.2vw, 32px);
    --chg-coda-k:      20.7752;
    --chg-coda-floor:  calc(1.5 * var(--bio-size));
    --chg-coda-window: calc(50vw - 76px);

    /* ---- THE TURN'S TWO GEOMETRY TOKENS, R9.6 -------------------
       Both were literals inside .chg-turn and both are now consumed
       by a SECOND element — the frame's top registration. CONTEXT.md
       R8.2: "a relationship that must hold is declared against its
       counterpart, never left to fall out of two swept values." The
       frame's offset is a function of the turn's air and the turn's
       leading; if either moves and the frame keeps a stale copy, the
       registration silently comes apart and nothing reports it.

       NEITHER VALUE CHANGES. --chg-turn-air is byte-for-byte the
       margin .chg-turn already carried (2.0 body line-heights, R9.2,
       settled) and --chg-turn-lh is the 1.3 it already carried. This
       is a re-declaration, not a re-derivation. */
    --chg-turn-air: calc(2.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    --chg-turn-lh:  1.3;

    /* ---- THE FRAME'S TOP REGISTRATION, R9.6 ---------------------
       The distance from the type row's top edge to the TURN'S CAP
       HEIGHT — which is where the frame's top trim now lands.

           register = turn air + half-leading + (ascent - cap height)

       stated in the turn's own em so it re-resolves from the type at
       every width with no breakpoint and nothing swept:

           half-leading      = (lh - asc - desc) / 2
           asc - cap         = asc - cap
           sum, in em        = lh/2 + (asc - desc)/2 - cap

       FRAUNCES' METRICS, MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. Read off
       CanvasRenderingContext2D.measureText() at ten sizes from 200px
       to 2000px, where Chrome's whole-pixel rounding of ascent and
       descent is negligible against the size:

           ascent      0.97750 em
           descent     0.25500 em
           cap height  0.70000 em

       The cap ratio lands on 0.700 exactly, at every one of the ten
       sizes and at all five shipped sizes — an independent
       confirmation of CONTEXT.md's R2 measurement, which was taken
       off rendered ink by a different method entirely.

           k = 1.3/2 + (0.9775 - 0.255)/2 - 0.700 = 0.31125

       WHY A MODELLED CONSTANT IS LEGAL HERE, AND WHY R6.3 DOES NOT
       FORBID IT. R6.3 rules that where elements must share a
       BASELINE, grid's baseline-sharing group answers and a modelled
       em offset must not. That route does not exist for this
       problem: CSS has no cap-height alignment, `align-self:
       baseline` aligns ALPHABETIC baselines, and the frame is a
       photograph with no baseline of its own to contribute to a
       group. A model is the only thing CSS can express here, which
       is the case R8.2's 3px ink tolerance was written for.

       AND THE MODEL IS VERIFIED AGAINST MEASURED INK, per width, not
       inferred from the arithmetic. Residual, frame top trim to turn
       cap height, at the five beside widths:

           1920  +0.06px      1279  -0.34px
           1440  -0.05px      1255  -0.03px
           1280  -0.33px

       Worst 0.34px, against a 3px tolerance that MUST NOT be
       tightened and against the 0.65px width-to-width jitter of
       Chrome's own rounding. The residual is smaller than the noise
       floor of the quantity it measures. */
    --chg-frame-register: calc(
      var(--chg-turn-air) + var(--closing-size) * 0.31125
    );
  }

  /* ---- THE HEAD --------------------------------------------------
     Top-aligned in its row so the kicker's first baseline sits on the
     section's top content edge, level with the lede's first line
     rather than floating in the middle of a tall row. */
  .chg-head{
    align-self: start;

    /* THE KICKER'S SIZE IS A TOKEN SO THE SUBTITLE CAN DERIVE FROM IT.
       (R9.1.) The kicker carries the page's optical half-tracking
       pullback — half of 0.26em, so the C's stem lands on the column
       line rather than on the tracking space after it — and the
       subtitle carried none, so the two sat 1.36px apart on rendered
       ink while their boxes looked identical in the source. That is
       R8.1's rule exactly: equalize on ink, not on box edges.

       The subtitle now takes the SAME pull-back IN PIXELS, derived
       from this token rather than restated: -0.13em against 10.5px is
       -1.365px, and calc(-0.13 x var(--chg-kicker-size)) is the same
       -1.365px whatever the subtitle's own font-size happens to be. An
       `em` pullback on the subtitle would have resolved against ITS
       12px and overshot by 0.195px. Declared once, consumed twice; if
       the kicker's size ever moves, the subtitle follows it. */
    --chg-kicker-size: 10.5px;
  }

  /* The kicker is an <h2>; the browser's heading margins and weight
     are reset here rather than inherited. Treatment matches the
     global .kicker primitive — 10.5px Montserrat, 0.26em tracking,
     uppercase, Smoked Slate — with the optical half-tracking pullback
     so the C's stem lands on the column line rather than on the
     tracking space after it.

     WEIGHT 500, matching .edu-kicker rather than the 400 of the
     global .kicker primitive: this string is a section name carrying
     an <h2>, and Educational Leader established that register for
     exactly that role. */
  .chg-kicker{
    margin: 0;
    margin-left: calc(-0.13 * var(--chg-kicker-size));
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: var(--chg-kicker-size);
    line-height: var(--baseline);
    letter-spacing: 0.26em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--slate);
  }

  /* THE BREAK IS AUTHORED, AND IT IS CONDITIONAL BY CONSTRUCTION.
     (R9.1.)

     R9 let the kicker rag, and in its two-column outdent it broke as
     "CHARGING IT TO THE / GAME" — a one-word widow under three words.
     The wanted break is "CHARGING IT / TO THE GAME": two halves of
     comparable length.

     WHY NOT A <br>. A literal <br> is unconditional, and the kicker
     sets on ONE line at 1920, 1023, 750, 390 and 360 — a <br> would
     force a break at all five, which is exactly what must not happen.
     Making it conditional needs the width at which the full string
     stops fitting, and that is TWO swept pixel boundaries derived from
     a string's rendered width — 1522 in bands 1-2 and 990 in band 3 —
     in a file whose standing rule is that swept boundaries go stale
     silently. There is also no single media query that expresses it:
     the string fits at 1023 and does not at 1024, so the boundary
     crosses a band edge in the wrong direction.

     Two nowrap halves with an ordinary space between them make the
     break FALL OUT of the fit instead of being scheduled:

       fits on one line   -> no break, at any width, ever
       does not fit       -> the only breakable point is the space
                             between the halves, so it breaks there
       three lines        -> structurally impossible; neither half can
                             break internally

     Each of the brief's three requirements is then met by
     construction rather than by a number that has to stay true.
     Measured: the wider half, "TO THE GAME", is 110.4px, against a
     narrowest kicker window of 129.3px at 1024 — so neither half ever
     overflows its column.

     THE ACCESSIBLE NAME IS UNAFFECTED. The two spans carry the same
     characters in the same order with the space between them, so the
     <h2> still computes to "Charging It to the Game" — the exact
     string sections.ts carries as the folio label. Verified, not
     assumed; see the R9.1 report. */
  .chg-kicker span{ white-space: nowrap; }

  /* The deck, in the register Educational Leader, Premier Leadership,
     Everyday Legends and Athletic Management all share. Sentence
     case, deliberately: the kicker above it is uppercase and
     letterspaced, so setting the subtitle the same way would give the
     head two identical-looking lines and no hierarchy. Set verbatim
     from COPY.md. */
  .chg-subtitle{
    margin: 2px 0 0;
    margin-left: calc(-0.13 * var(--chg-kicker-size));
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.012em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---- THE TYPE COLUMN -------------------------------------------
     TOP-ALIGNED. R9.1 put this column and the figure into a grid
     baseline-sharing group so the caption's last baseline met the
     coda's, and that was a mistake with a name: it applied R6.3's
     surplus rule to a case that is not R6.3's.

     ON ATHLETIC THE FRAME IS TALLER THAN THE TYPE. The surplus exists
     whatever you do, and the only question is where it lands — above
     the type, per R6.3, rather than inside a gap the type derived.

     HERE THE TYPE COLUMN IS TALLER. There was no surplus to place.
     Bottom-aligning the figure did not move a hole, it MANUFACTURED
     one: 347px of empty ground at 1440 and 504px at 1142, opened
     beside the largest type on the page, to close a 72.5px gap at the
     foot. A hole five times the size of the one it fixed, in the more
     conspicuous place.

     Both columns are back on `start`. The frame's top trim sits on the
     section's top content edge, the caption sits beneath the frame,
     and the figure column simply runs shorter than the type — which
     is what a column plate beside a longer text column does in print.
     Which column governs is MEASURED at every width and reported; it
     is not assumed.

     ---- R9.5: WHICH COLUMN GOVERNS HAS NOW INVERTED, AND THE SURPLUS
     RULE CANNOT BE APPLIED. REPORTED, NOT ARBITRATED.

     With the lede lifted out of this column into its own band, the
     frame is TALLER than the type beside it at every beside width, for
     the first time in this section. Measured, frame minus type:

         1920   +309.42px        1280    +9.72px
         1440    +57.36px        1255    +2.19px

     R6.3 says a shape floor's surplus lands ABOVE the type column —
     bottom-align it. Built and measured, that is what it does here:

         width   lede -> turn gap   in body line-heights   8-line ceiling
         1920        370.61px             12.11              BREACH
         1440        118.55px              3.87              ok
         1280         70.91px              2.32              ok
         1255         63.38px              2.07              ok

     12.11 line-heights at 1920 is CONTEXT.md's own documented failure,
     reproduced exactly — "it blew the 8-line gap ceiling at 1920 twice
     (R6.2 at 13.39 lines, and R6.3's first arrangement)", and "the
     failure only appears at the widest viewports, where the floor's
     surplus is largest, so it will not show at 1440." It did not show
     at 1440 here either. It showed at 1920 and nowhere else.

     WHY THE RULE DOES NOT TRANSFER. On Athletic the type column STARTS
     the section — kicker first — so "above the type column" is the
     section's own top margin, which has no ceiling on it. Here the
     lede band sits above the type column in the same columns, so
     "above the type column" IS a gap between two type blocks, which
     R6.3 forbids in the same breath. The surplus has no legal
     destination above the type in this arrangement.

     LEFT ON `start`, WHICH CHANGES NOTHING THAT WAS ALREADY SHIPPING.
     The lede -> turn gap stays at a flat 61.19px (2.00 body lines) at
     every beside width and the frame overhangs the coda instead. This
     is not a ruling that the overhang is correct; it is the only
     option that does not breach a documented ceiling, and the conflict
     is in the R9.5 report for a decision. */
  .chg-text{
    align-self: start;
  }

  /* ---- LEDE ------------------------------------------------------
     THE PAGE'S SHARED --lede-size. FLAT, IN BOTH ARRANGEMENTS, WITH
     NOTHING DERIVED. (R9.5.)

     EVERY CUSTOM PROPERTY, CAP, RAG RATIO AND MEASURED CONSTANT
     INTRODUCED FOR THIS LEDE ACROSS R9.2, R9.3 AND R9.4 IS DELETED.
     By name: --chg-lede-k (42.3942, the measured advance-per-px),
     --chg-lede-window (declared twice — calc(50vw - 76px) in bands 1-2
     and calc(100vw - 80px) in band 3), the 0.8973 rag ratio and its
     0.96 headroom folded into the 0.861 constant, the four-line cap
     the constant expressed, the 3 x body floor beneath it, and the
     band-2 font-size override that switched the whole derivation off
     below the crossover. Also gone: the band-3 `width` override and
     the band-4 `width: auto` reset, both of which existed only to keep
     the box and the derived size in agreement.

     THEY WERE ALL SOLVING THE SAME WRONG PROBLEM. The lede ran long
     because it was set in a six-column body measure, not because
     --lede-size is too large. R9.2 capped it at three lines and hit
     CONTEXT.md's 3x lede-to-body minimum; R9.3 scoped the cap to the
     beside arrangement; R9.4 relaxed the cap to four lines and raised
     the floor. Three passes, each moving the same imbalance somewhere
     else, none of them touching the window. Widen the window and every
     one of those constants has nothing left to do.

     WHAT THAT BUYS, MECHANICALLY: nothing in this section now carries
     a swept constant for the lede, so there is nothing to go stale
     when the copy, the face, the axes or the crossover moves. The lede
     is declared against the same token About, Educational Leader,
     Premier Leadership and Everyday Legends declare, and it changes
     when that token changes.

     NO max-width, NO width, NO clamp of its own, NO line cap, NO <br>.
     Measure is controlled by the column span and by nothing else —
     CONTEXT.md's Layout System rule, which the derived width overrides
     were quietly working around.

     text-wrap: balance is KEPT. It is what .prem-lede, .legends-lede
     and .edu-lede all carry, it changes no line COUNT, and an engine
     without it wraps normally. */
  .chg-lede{
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-lede);
    font-weight: 350;
    font-size: var(--lede-size);
    line-height: var(--lede-lh);
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- THE TURN --------------------------------------------------
     THE HINGE, AND IT IS NOW SET LIKE ONE. (R9.2.)

     R9 set this at body size, on the reasoning that space alone should
     set it apart and that promoting it would collide with the pivot.
     The first half held; the second was over-cautious. At 17px, alone
     in 122px of air on both sides, with a 54.72px lede above it and a
     31.68px coda below, it read as a caption that had lost its
     picture — the smallest type in the section sitting in the largest
     space in the section.

     IT TAKES THE PAGE'S CLOSING-LINE REGISTER — the same
     --closing-size and the same four axes the coda uses, opsz 96 /
     wght 400 / SOFT 35 / WONK 0. NO NEW TOKEN. NOT Fraunces italic:
     that is the pivot's, and WONK 1 is spent there and nowhere else.
     The turn and the coda now share a register, which is right — they
     are the section's two statements, and the body and the lede are
     what they turn between.

     THE AIR COMES DOWN, from four body line-heights to two. (Item 4.)
     122.4px of air on both sides was sized against a five-line lede
     and a body-size turn; with the lede capped at three lines and the
     turn at display-adjacent scale, the same air made the section
     mostly empty. calc(2.0 x 17px x 1.8) = 61.2px.

     IT IS STILL THE SECTION'S MOST-AIRED ELEMENT BY A CLEAR MARGIN,
     which is the requirement that has not moved: 61.2px against the
     next largest gap of 36.7px, a ratio of 1.67. Stated in the body's
     own tokens so it re-resolves from the type at every width with no
     breakpoint.

     THE TURN MUST NOT WRAP. 40 characters at 13.6232 advance-per-px
     on these axes; fit is reported at all thirteen widths. Where it
     does not fit, that is REPORTED — it is not shrunk below the
     register. */
  .chg-turn{
    margin: var(--chg-turn-air) 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 96, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 35, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--closing-size);
    line-height: var(--chg-turn-lh);
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* ---- BODY ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 3 of 3, on --bio-size / --bio-lh, the page's body tokens,
     unchanged. One paragraph, per COPY.md. NO max-width — span only.

     BODY -> CODA. 1.2 body lines = 36.72px, down from 2.0 in R9.
     (Item 4.) Educational Leader marks the same relationship with a
     hairline rule; this section has no rules by design, so the gap
     carries it alone — but it only has to say "the text block has
     ended", and at 61.2px it was saying it as loudly as the turn's
     air, which has to say something bigger. It is now the section's
     SECOND largest gap, at 0.6 of the turn's. */
  .chg-body{
    margin: 0 0 calc(1.2 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: var(--bio-size);
    line-height: var(--bio-lh);
    letter-spacing: 0.004em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    orphans: 3;
    widows: 3;
  }

  .chg-body p{ margin: 0; }

  /* ---- THE CODA --------------------------------------------------
     THE SIZE DERIVES FROM ITS OWN LONGEST LINE. (R9.1, correcting R9.)

     R9 reused the page's shared closing-line register unchanged —
     clamp(26px, 2.2vw, 32px) — and line 3 wrapped at 8 of the 13
     widths. The register is not wrong; it was derived on Educational
     Leader and Athletic Management, whose closings are ONE line each.
     This is the page's only three-line coda and its longest line is 53
     characters. A size derived for a different job does not transfer,
     and holding the token fixed was holding the wrong thing fixed.

     THE CONSTANT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. Rendered at the shipped
     axes — opsz 96, wght 400, SOFT 35, WONK 0, tracking -0.012em —
     line 3 measures 2077.516px at 100px, so it costs

         K = 20.7752 px of width per px of type

     and K IS GENUINELY CONSTANT: 20.7752 at 26px, 20.7754 at 40px,
     20.7752 at 100px. It has to be — `opsz` is PINNED at 96 rather
     than swept, so the face does not change shape with size, and the
     tracking is stated in `em` so it scales with it. A face whose
     optical size varied would not admit a constant at all. (Brief's
     figure 20.78; measured 20.7752, agreeing to 0.02%.)

     THE THREE TERMS:

       cap    --closing-size, the shared register. The coda MUST NOT
              exceed it. This is a correction for FIT, not a promotion
              in the hierarchy — the coda stays tier 2 of 3, under the
              lede and over the body.
       fit    window / K, with 4% headroom.
       floor  1.5 x body = 25.5px. Below this it stops reading as a
              coda and the failure has moved rather than closed, so
              the floor is a REPORTING TRIPWIRE, not a safety net:
              wherever it engages, the width is reported as a failure.

     --closing-size IS A LOCAL ALIAS, NOT A NEW TOKEN. It names the
     value .edu-coda and .ath-close already carry so that min() can
     refer to it. It is declared on .chg and inherited by nothing else
     on the page; neither of those components is touched.

     THE WINDOW IS DECLARED PER BAND, in closed form against the same
     grid arithmetic the primitives use, and each formula is verified
     against the rendered window at four widths in its own band. Band 3
     also SETS its width from the same custom property, so the size and
     the box cannot disagree — the quantity is declared once and used
     twice rather than swept twice.

     text-wrap: balance is DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and prohibited: these
     lines are authored breaks and asking the browser to balance them
     is asking it to re-break lines whose breaks are the design. So are
     hyphenation and any tightening of the tracking. */
  .chg-coda p{
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 96, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 35, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: max(
      var(--chg-coda-floor),
      min(
        var(--closing-size),
        calc(var(--chg-coda-window) / var(--chg-coda-k) * 0.96)
      )
    );
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* THE AUTHORED BREAK, BAND 4 ONLY.

     340px cannot hold a 53-character line at any size that still
     reads as a coda, and that is not closable by geometry — at 390
     the exact-fit size is 25.43px against a 25.5px floor. The design
     intent is that the coda's breaks are CHOSEN, so a chosen break at
     a measure that cannot hold the line honours that intent where a
     ragged wrap does not.

     ONE break, after "simply", nowhere else, no other line, no other
     band. Not a word of the copy changes.

     THE SPACE BEFORE THE <br> IS LOAD-BEARING. Above 750 the element
     is display: none and the two text nodes rejoin; without that
     space they would rejoin as "simplyhave". The trailing space
     collapses at the end of a broken line, so it costs nothing when
     the break is live. */
  .chg-coda-break{ display: none; }

  /* ---- THE FRAME -------------------------------------------------
     Top-aligned with the type — see .chg-text for why the R9.1
     baseline group was reverted rather than tuned. The caption sits
     beneath the frame and the column's shorter run below it is
     ordinary, not a defect.

     THE FRAME'S OWN GEOMETRY IS UNTOUCHED. No height floor, no
     stretch, no change of aspect, no change of crop: the plate keeps
     the 0.800 shape R9 derived at 1920 and the 112% / -12% window. The
     distance is closed by moving the column, not by resizing the
     photograph. */
  /* ---- THE FRAME'S TOP TRIM IS REGISTERED TO THE TURN'S CAP HEIGHT
     (R9.6.) -------------------------------------------------------

     WHAT WAS WRONG. R9.5 put the frame in row 2 and left it at the
     row's top edge, which is the LEDE'S BOTTOM. Measured, the lede's
     last line box did not merely touch the plate — it OVERLAPPED it:

         1920  -1.48px      1279  -0.64px
         1440  -1.44px      1255  -0.64px
         1280  -0.64px

     A display line running out over columns 9-11 with the plate
     starting under it at negative clearance is not a composition; it
     is two elements colliding. And the row's top edge is not a
     registration mark for anything a reader can see: it is the
     lede's descender line, which is invisible.

     WHAT IT REGISTERS TO NOW. The turn's CAP HEIGHT — the top of the
     "B" in "But this is not simply a football story." That is a mark
     the reader can actually see, it is the first ink of the column
     the plate stands beside, and a plate top trim level with the
     first cap of the text beside it is the ordinary editorial
     registration.

     NOT THE TURN'S BOX TOP. The box carries --chg-turn-air above it
     (2.0 body line-heights, settled R9.2) and its own half-leading
     above the cap. Registering to the box would land the trim
     61.19px + a leading above the ink and reproduce the same defect
     lower down the page. The offset is derived in --chg-frame-register
     against the turn's own type; see its declaration on .chg.

     THE STACKED BANDS OVERRIDE THIS MARGIN OUTRIGHT. Below the
     crossover the frame leaves the type's row entirely and the
     `@media (max-width: 1254px)` rule below replaces margin-top with
     its own value, so nothing here reaches any stacked band. Verified
     by measuring both trees, not assumed. */
  .chg-figure{
    margin: var(--chg-frame-register) 0 0;
    align-self: start;
  }

  /* ---- THE STACKED BANDS ONLY: air between the coda and the frame
     Below the crossover the frame leaves the type's row and lands in
     a row of its own beneath the coda, and grid gives a new row no
     gap at all — the sheet declares column-gap and nothing else. Built
     without this the photograph's top trim sat hard against the coda's
     last line at 1141 and below, which reads as the coda captioning
     the picture rather than as the section closing and a plate
     following it.

     THE VALUE IS FORCED, NOT CHOSEN — it is the only body-line
     multiple that fits between two constraints already in this file:

       > 2 lines (61.2px)   the largest gap INSIDE the type block, the
                            body -> coda gap. A gap to the photograph
                            no bigger than a gap inside the column
                            makes the photograph read as one more block
                            in the column.
       < 4 lines (122.4px)  the turn's air. That is the section's
                            largest gap BY DESIGN and it is the only
                            thing setting the turn alone; anything
                            matching or exceeding it takes the device
                            away.

     Three body lines — calc(3.0 x 17px x 1.8) = 91.8px — is the only
     integer multiple strictly between them. Stated in the body's own
     tokens so it re-resolves from the type at every width, like every
     other gap in this section.

     IT IS SCOPED TO THE STACKED BANDS AND MUST NOT BE MADE
     UNCONDITIONAL. In the beside arrangement the frame shares the
     type's row and its top trim IS the section's top content edge,
     level with the kicker and the lede; a margin there would push the
     photograph down off that edge for no reason. */
  /* THE SHAPE. One intrinsic aspect at every width — no height: 100%,
     no padding-top spacer, no aspect floor. Athletic's frame needs a
     minimum shape because it is the page's spread moment and must
     hold a trim; this one is a plate in a column and holds nothing.
     `aspect-ratio` is exactly the right tool when the requirement is
     a shape rather than a minimum, which is the distinction R6 drew
     when it had to reject it there.

     THE CROP, AND WHY IT IS A VERTICAL WINDOW.

     Source: 1500 x 2100 — exactly 5:7, native aspect 0.714286. The
     rendered aspect is WIDER than native, so the crop is a
     horizontal band on the full source width: the sides stay, and
     the window comes off the TOP. Nothing is cut from the left, the
     right, or the bottom.

     THE TOP IS WHERE IT COMES OFF, AND THAT IS AN EDITORIAL CHOICE
     BEFORE IT IS A GEOMETRIC ONE. The open sky is the most
     palette-hostile region in the file — it is the one saturated
     blue in a frame that also has to agree with Antique Gold and
     Cashmere — and it is also the least informative part of the
     photograph. Taking the window off the top removes it, brings the
     tree line down to just above her, and keeps every part of the
     frame that makes this the page's one ENVIRONMENTAL image: the
     receding street, the traffic signals, the storefronts, the
     road markings she is standing on. Taking the same amount off the
     bottom would have cut her at the thigh and kept the sky, which
     is the trade backwards.

     The window's height, as a fraction of the source:

       f = native aspect / rendered aspect = (5/7) / (4/5) = 25/28

     and the two crop terms follow from f alone:

       --crop-h  =  1 / f            = 28/25  = 112.000%
       --crop-t  = -( 1 - f ) / f    = -3/25  =  -12.000%

     Both exact, because 5:7 and 4:5 are both exact. BOTH ARE
     PERCENTAGES OF THE PLATE'S OWN HEIGHT, and the plate's height is
     its width divided by a fixed aspect, so plate size cancels out
     of the source window entirely and the crop is IDENTICAL in all
     four bands — no drift, no breakpoint, nothing to re-measure.
     Narrowing the plate therefore SCALES the image; it does not
     tighten the crop. This is CONTEXT.md's R4.1 finding transposed
     one axis: there the invariant terms were percentages of width
     because the window was horizontal, here they are percentages of
     height because the window is vertical, and in both cases the
     property being protected is that plate size cancels.

     NO TRANSFORM ON THE PHOTOGRAPH. A transform promotes the <img>
     to its own compositing layer and .plate-img::before silently
     stops blending against it — measured on this project at R-B 8 to
     -1, the multiply simply gone. The oversized box inside
     .plate-img's overflow: hidden is how the crop is done.

     WHY 0.80 AND NOT SOMETHING TALLER. Two constraints close on this
     number from opposite sides and there is not much room between
     them.

     From below, THE TYPE MUST GOVERN THE SECTION'S HEIGHT. The type
     column gets SHORTER as the viewport widens (the measure widens,
     the paragraph loses lines) while a fixed-aspect frame gets
     TALLER, so the two cross at some width for any aspect. Measured
     at 1920, the widest verified width and the worst case: the type
     column is 855.2px, so the frame plus its caption must fit in
     that, and the caption is 56px of it. 581.3 / (855.2 - 56) =
     0.7273 is the floor. A first pass shipped 0.62 and put the frame
     138.4px TALLER than the type at 1920 — the section's height set
     by the photograph, with the coda closing early and the plate
     running past it. That failure appears at 1920 and nowhere else,
     which is the third time on this project that a defect has lived
     only above 1440 (CONTEXT.md, R8.2).

     From above, NO TWO PHOTOGRAPHS ON THE PAGE SHARE A CROP. The two
     other FIXED shapes on the page are athletics-detail at 0.70 and
     Athletic's frame at 1.00; Premier's plate and the Cover's are
     row-driven and sweep 0.62-1.05 and 0.59-1.11, so they are not
     shapes to be distinct from so much as ranges that any fixed
     value crosses somewhere. 0.80 clears 0.70 by 14% and 1.00 by
     20%, and it is the roundest value in the window the floor
     leaves.

     It is still a PORTRAIT — 4:5, taller than wide. CONTEXT.md's
     rule is that no vertical image is ever cropped WIDE, and this
     one is not: it is cropped from 5:7 to 4:5 and stays upright. */
  .chg-frame{
    aspect-ratio: var(--chg-aspect);
    --chg-aspect: 0.80;
    --crop-h: 112%;
    --crop-t: -12%;
  }

  /* :global() — <Image> emits its own <img>, which never receives
     Astro's data-astro-cid-*, so a scoped selector silently stops
     matching. This has bitten this project twice.

     max-width: none is not optional. Tailwind's preflight sets
     `img { max-width: 100% }`, which clamps the oversized box back to
     the plate width and produces no crop at all. */
  .chg-frame :global(img){
    position: absolute;
    top: var(--crop-t);
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: none;
    height: var(--crop-h);
    object-fit: cover;
  }


  @media (max-width: 1254px){
    .chg-figure{
      margin-top: calc(3.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    }

    /* BAND 2 BELOW THE CROSSOVER.

       THE TYPE WINDOW NARROWED FROM 3-10 TO 3-9 IN THIS PASS. R9.2
       released the crossover for re-derivation, and the 78-character
       ceiling on the stacked measure was exactly what pinned its upper
       bracket; buying headroom there is what let the crossover rise to
       where the coda fits in the BESIDE arrangement. Seven columns:
           7 cols + 6 gaps = 7(100vw - 392px)/12 + 144px
                           = 58.3333vw - 84.667px
       The lede takes the same window here.

       THE CODA RUNS TO THE RIGHT TRIM HERE TOO — columns 3-12, from
       the type's own left edge. It has a fit requirement the type does
       not, and band 3 already establishes the coda as the one element
       in this section allowed its own wider window; doing the same
       here makes that a RULE — in every stacked band the coda runs to
       the right trim — rather than a one-off.

       It also removes a sub-window this band would otherwise need. At
       3-9 the coda falls under its 529.77px floor requirement below
       1053; at 3-10 the type breaches 78 characters above 1147. No
       single shared window spans [1024, crossover], so the coda takes
       its own:
           10 cols + 9 gaps = 10(100vw - 392px)/12 + 216px
                            = 83.3333vw - 110.667px */
    .chg{
      --chg-coda-window: calc(83.3333vw - 110.667px);
    }
    .chg-coda{
      width: var(--chg-coda-window);
    }

    /* NO .chg-lede RULE HERE ANY MORE (R9.5). This block used to
       switch the lede's derivation off below the crossover by
       redeclaring font-size as --lede-size. The derivation is gone, so
       the override has nothing to override: the lede takes --lede-size
       in band 1 and inherits it down. An override that agrees with the
       default by coincidence is exactly the shape CONTEXT.md's R-shell
       addendum deletes rather than leaves standing. */
  }

  /* ---- BAND 3 · 751-1023 -----------------------------------------
     THE KICKER STOPS OUTDENTING, AND THE SECTION RUNS ON ONE LEFT
     EDGE. (R9.2, item 5.)

     R9.1 could not satisfy both of its own band-3 requirements: the
     coda had to run wider than the type in order to fit, and the type
     edge had to hold. Those two are only in conflict WHILE THE KICKER
     IS OUTDENTED — the outdent is what pushes the type off column 1
     and leaves the coda a different edge to run to.

     Drop the outdent and the conflict disappears. Kicker, subtitle,
     lede, turn, body and coda ALL START AT COLUMN 1: one left edge for
     the whole section in this band, no fourth edge, nothing to
     reconcile. The staircase is a desktop-space device, and an 8-track
     sheet at 751px does not have width to spend a step on it — the
     same reasoning that collapses it entirely in band 4, applied one
     band earlier.

     RIGHT EDGES DO THE DIFFERENTIATING INSTEAD, which is CONTEXT.md's
     R4.1 note used deliberately: the LEDE and the CODA run to the
     sheet's right trim, and the BODY stops short of it to hold its
     measure inside the 45-78 window. Two right edges, one left edge.

     THE BODY'S WINDOW IS UNCHANGED IN WIDTH — five columns above 900,
     six at 900 and below, exactly what R9.1 shipped. Only its left
     edge moved, from column 3 to column 1, so every measured character
     count is identical.

     THE HEAD NOW SITS ABOVE THE LEDE rather than beside it, so it
     needs the vertical gap it never needed while outdented: one body
     line, PremierLeadership's value for this pair, the same rule band
     4 already carried. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px){
    /* THE LEDE TAKES ITS WIDTH FROM ITS SPAN (R9.5). It used to carry
       a --chg-lede-window box override so it could run past the type's
       span to the sheet's right trim; the primitive .win-chg-lede now
       carries that placement itself, so the property is deleted rather
       than left declared for one consumer. The CODA still takes a box
       width here — that is settled R9.2 work and is untouched. */
    .chg{
      --chg-coda-window: calc(100vw - 80px);
    }
    .chg-head{
      margin-bottom: calc(1.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    }
    .chg-coda{
      width: var(--chg-coda-window);
      margin-left: 0;
    }
  }

  /* ---- BAND 4 · <= 750 -------------------------------------------
     Single column, full measure. The head keeps the gap band 3
     introduced — same pair, same relationship, one band further down.

     The coda no longer needs a box wider than its parent, because its
     parent is the measure. The lede never carries a box width at any
     band as of R9.5 — its span is its measure everywhere. */
  @media (max-width: 750px){
    .chg-head{
      margin-bottom: calc(1.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    }

    .chg-coda{
      width: auto;
      margin-left: 0;
    }

    /* THE WINDOW is the sheet's content box — the viewport less the
       clamped page gutter each side. Verified: 670.0 at 750, 340.1 at
       390, 312.0 at 360.

       THE CONSTANT CHANGES WITH THE STRING, AND THE STRING CHANGED IN
